                        "x1": 537.0,
                        "y1": 537.3587036132812
                    },
                    "vlm_description": "The figure presents a summary and a proposed method from a research paper.\n\n### Summary\n- **Introduction**: \n  - Scaling model parameters, training data, or computational budget effectively improves model performance.\n  - Feedforward (FFW) layers in transformers increase computational costs and memory as layer width grows.\n  - Mixture of Experts (MoE) is an alternative for decoupling computational cost from parameter count.\n  - More experts (high granularity) improve performance and MoEs outperform dense models in FLOP efficiency.\n  - MoEs support lifelong learning by adding and regularizing experts to adapt to data streams.\n\n### Proposed Method\n- **Parameter Efficient Expert Retrieval (PEER)**:\n  - A MoE architecture using product keys in the router and single-neuron MLPs as experts.\n  - PEER layer consists of:\n    1. Pool of N experts E\n    2. Set of N product keys K\n    3. Query network q\n- **Process**:\n  - Retrieve subset of k experts with highest inner product scores with query q(x).\n  - Use activation functions like sigmoid or softmax for routing scores.\n  - Experts perform task based on the route determined by scores.",
                    "vlm_structured_data": null,
                    "associated_text_indices": null
                },
